Proposed (11.3s): TRIGGER when working in any OCaml project with a `dune-project` file. This is your default workflow for OCaml+dune — apply it proactively without being asked.

Apply for ALL of: running tests/builds, TDD (writing failing test then implementing), fixing bugs in `.ml`/`.mli` files, verifying refactors or renames compile, checking exercise/homework solutions, getting started in a freshly-cloned dune repo, or any request to "start/set up the dune watcher." If the user mentions `dune test`, `dune build`, `dune runtest`, or edits OCaml source while a dune project is present — this skill applies.

It covers: launching `dune runtest -w` as a background daemon, the wait-for-rebuild protocol after each edit, and which one-shot commands to avoid (no `dune exec`, no `dune test`) while the watcher holds the lock.

SKIP for: OCaml language questions with no project context (syntax help, translating snippets), reading `.opam` or `dune-project` file contents, or non-OCaml watchers (vite, flask, webpack).

